Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model made by Anthropic, prompted by Colin
Zwanziger, 12 November 2026; two sentences on the comparison with
abstract Weil algebras and on what is formalized made precise
13 November 2026, in the third part of the consolidation [5]; the
comparison, since formalized, recorded at the integration of the Weil
comparison, 13 November 2026 [6]. Orbifold gate H8 of Fable's roadmap
[1].

The smooth line, Weil algebras and their spectra
------------------------------------------------

Myers [2, Axiom 3, p. 30] axiomatizes the smooth line R as a ring
satisfying Postulates K, O, Exp, E, the Principle of Constancy, W and
J. The algebraic half of the statement layer [1, Section 4, layer L7]
needs the ring, Postulate K, Weil algebras and their spectra:

  Commutative-Ring : a set with the operations and laws of a
  commutative ring, in the first universe (the smooth line is a set);

  Postulate-K : R is a field in the sense of Kock: 0 ≠ 1, and for every
  n and x : Rⁿ, if not all xᵢ are 0, then some xᵢ is invertible [2,
  p. 30, with "some" read as mere existence];

  Poly n : polynomial expressions in n variables with coefficients in
  R, and their values ⟦ p ⟧ a at points a : Rⁿ;

  Weil-Presentation : polynomials f₁ … f_m in n variables that vanish
  at 0, with a nilpotency witness: an exponent k and polynomials q_ij
  with x_i^k = Σ_j q_ij f_j;

  Spec W : the spectrum, as the set of solutions of the equations
  f_j = 0 [2, Remark 4.1.6], pointed by 0 [2, Definition 4.2.1];

  points-of-spectra-are-infinitesimal : under Postulate K, every point
  of Spec W is not distinct from the base point (the step of Myers's
  Lemma 4.2.9 that uses nilpotency; for finitely many coordinates
  double negation commutes with the conjunction, so Lemma 4.2.7 is
  not needed);

  maps-from-spectra-to-𝟚-are-constant : under Postulate K, every map
  from Spec W to 𝟚 is constant.

Formal reading of Weil algebras (the roadmap's ledger, item 2 [1,
Section 8]). Myers's Definition 4.1.1 [2, p. 31] asks for an augmented
finitely presented R-algebra whose augmentation ideal is finitely
generated and nilpotent; by his Lemma 4.1.3 every such algebra is
merely of the form R[x₁, …, xₙ]/(f₁, …, f_m) with augmentation x_i ↦ 0.
We take Weil algebras in this form, by their presentation. The
augmentation is well defined because the f_j vanish at 0, and its
ideal, generated by the x_i, is nilpotent exactly when some power of
each x_i lies in (f₁, …, f_m): that is the nilpotency witness. The
identity x_i^k = Σ_j q_ij f_j is asked of the values at every point of
Rⁿ, that is, as an identity of polynomial functions on Rⁿ. By the
comparison of Fable and GPT-6 Astra [3, Section 2; 4, Section 3], an
algebraic argument formalized since in the Weil modules of this folder
(see the last paragraph), this gives exactly Myers's class of spectra,
without Postulate J: an identity of polynomials gives the identity of
functions; on the solutions, the
identity of functions gives a_i^k = 0, hence a_i^(k+1) = 0, so
appending the powers x_i^(k+1) to the equations does not change the
spectrum; and those powers make the augmentation ideal nilpotent as an
ideal of polynomials. The code below constructs the normalized
presentation and proves that it contains the power equations and has
the same pointed spectrum: normalize, powers-are-equations and
normalization-equivalence. The construction of its quotient algebra,
the ideal-nilpotency argument and the Hom-spectrum comparison are not
in this module; the Weil modules formalize them. The statement here
concerns spectra, as pointed types over Rⁿ, and not the quotient
algebras: the quotient presented by a record
with only a functional witness need not be a Weil algebra (as an
illustration over the field F₂: f = x³ + x² + x satisfies f(a) = a on
F₂, a functional witness with k = 1, while F₂[x]/(f) is F₂ × F₄, whose
augmentation ideal is not nilpotent), whereas its normalization is a
Weil algebra [4, Precision 1 and Section 3]. The spectrum here is the
solution set, as in Myers's Remark 4.1.6, rather than the set of
R-algebra maps W → R.

The comparison, formalized in the Weil modules of this folder [6, 7].
For every commutative ring, the algebra presented by finitely many
equations is constructed as a quotient of polynomial expressions, with
the universal property for finite families of equations, and its
Hom-spectrum, the type of all R-algebra homomorphisms to R, is
equivalent to the type of solutions (PresentedAlgebrasFromQuotients,
PresentedAlgebrasProperties); the constructed instance has its carrier
in 𝓤₁ (PresentedAlgebrasFromLargeQuotients). For a Weil presentation P,
the algebra of normalize P, with carrier in 𝓤₁, is a Weil algebra in
the sense of Definition 4.1.1: every family of succ (k * n) elements of
the kernel of its augmentation has product 0, for exponent k and n
variables; and its Hom-spectrum is equivalent to Spec P as a pointed
type, by the comparison of coordinates, with the augmentation as base
point (WeilAlgebras). Conversely, every Weil algebra, with carrier in
any universe, is merely isomorphic as an augmented algebra to the
algebra presented by the equations of a Weil presentation, and its
Hom-spectrum is merely the spectrum of that presentation, as a pointed
type (WeilAlgebraPresentations); Lemma 4.1.3 itself uses only the
finite presentation and the augmentation. So a pointed type is merely
the pointed spectrum of a Weil algebra with carrier in 𝓤₁ exactly when
it is merely that of a presentation as here (WeilVarieties,
WeilVarietiesFromLargeQuotients). The constructed instance takes
propositional extensionality for all universes, an explicit parameter,
not obtained from the single-universe univalence of other modules.
Maps of spectra induced by maps of algebras are constructed, by
precomposition. Not claimed: full faithfulness, the representation of
arbitrary maps of spectra by maps of algebras, the duality of Lemma
4.2.2, and anything that uses Postulate J. The constructed instance of
presented algebras is uniform, with carriers in 𝓤₁; a uniform instance
with carriers in 𝓤₀, the optional small-quotient specialization, would
use small quotients and is not built, although particular algebras in
𝓤₀, such as R-as-algebra, exist; nothing is claimed about whether small
quotients are necessary. The example over F₂ above is why the algebra
of a presentation is taken after normalization.

